Investigate this Wazuh alert:

{
  "severity": "critical",
  "host_observations": [
    {"host": "WS-DEV-22.corp.local", "role": "endpoint", "user": "j.lopez", "first_seen": "2032-07-30T14:08:08+0000", "last_seen": "2032-07-30T14:13:00+0000"}
  ],
  "attack_progression": [
    {"step": 1, "tactic": "LDAP enumeration of CN=Enrollment Services", "technique": "T1649", "what_happened": "At 2032-07-30 14:08:08 UTC on WS-DEV-22, AMSI EID 4104 captured a powershell.exe scriptblock issuing LDAP query against 'CN=Enrollment Services,CN=Public Key Services,CN=Services,<configNC>' with filter '(objectClass=pKIEnrollmentService)' returning cn, dNSHostName, certificateTemplates. Rule 100612 triggered.", "chain_reason": "LDAP enumeration of pKIEnrollmentService objects is how an attacker discovers all CA servers and certificate templates in the AD forest — the prerequisite step of ESC1/ESC2/.../ESC8 abuse."},
    {"step": 2, "tactic": "Certificate request with SAN abuse (ESC1)", "technique": "T1649", "what_happened": "At 2032-07-30 14:08:42, Sysmon EID 1 captured 'certreq.exe -New' with an INF file at %TEMP%\\p145_adcs_140808\\esc1_req.inf. AMSI EID 4104 captured the INF file content with extension '2.5.29.17 = \"{text}upn=Administrator@corp.local\"' (the Microsoft UPN OID 1.3.6.1.4.1.311.20.2.3 inside the Subject Alternative Name section) AND Subject='CN=legit-user' (the LOW-PRIVILEGE user requesting). Rule 100610 + 100611 triggered.", "chain_reason": "ESC1 abuse — the vulnerable template has CT_FLAG_ENROLLEE_SUPPLIES_SUBJECT enabled, allowing the low-priv requester to insert ANY UPN they choose into the SAN. AD PKINIT then treats the SAN UPN as the AUTHENTICATED identity — so the cert will authenticate AS Administrator."},
    {"step": 3, "tactic": "ESC8 NTLM relay probe to AD CS HTTP enrollment endpoint", "technique": "T1649", "what_happened": "At 2032-07-30 14:09:38, Sysmon EID 3 captured outbound HTTP (port 80) from powershell.exe to http://adcs01.corp.local/certsrv/ with Authorization: Negotiate header (UseDefaultCredentials). Probe response 401 challenge — confirms the AD CS web enrollment endpoint is reachable AND accepts NTLM authentication (the vulnerable configuration for ESC8 NTLM relay).", "chain_reason": "AD CS HTTP web enrollment accepting NTLM is the ESC8 precondition. A reachable /certsrv/ with NTLM enables relaying a coerced DC$ machine-account auth to obtain a domain controller certificate."},
    {"step": 4, "tactic": "Tool literal: Certify / Certipy / Rubeus PKINIT", "technique": "T1649", "what_happened": "AMSI EID 4104 scriptblock content includes literal command templates: 'Certify.exe find /vulnerable', 'Certify.exe request /ca:corp-adcs01\\corp-CA /template:VulnTemplate /altname:Administrator', 'certipy req -u lowuser@corp.local -p pass -ca corp-CA -template VulnTemplate -upn Administrator@corp.local', 'certipy auth -pfx admin.pfx -dc-ip 10.108.57.10', 'Rubeus.exe asktgt /user:Administrator /certificate:admin.pfx /password:p'. Rule 100612 fired with high confidence.", "chain_reason": "Literal 'Certify.exe find /vulnerable' OR 'certipy req' OR 'Rubeus asktgt /certificate:' in a PowerShell scriptblock are signature offensive tooling — these tool names have no legitimate use in any corp script."}
  ],
  "time_window": {"start": "2032-07-30T14:08:08+0000", "end": "2032-07-30T14:13:00+0000"},
  "alert_count": 4,
  "unique_steps": 4
}
